### What is the problem?

Given a string, find the minimum number of characters to insert anywhere to make the entire string a palindrome. For "abcda", insert 'c' and 'b' to get "abcdcba", so the answer is 2.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class MinInsertionsPalindrome {
    public static int minInsertions(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[n][n];
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            dp[i][i] = 1;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j)) {
                    dp[i][j] = 2 + dp[i + 1][j - 1];
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
                }
            }
        }
        return n - dp[0][n - 1];
    }
}
```

### Why does this reduce to the LPS?

Characters that belong to the longest palindromic subsequence already have their symmetric partners within the string. They can stay in place. Every other character is unmatched and needs a mirror partner inserted on the other side. So the number of insertions equals the number of unmatched characters, which is `n - LPS`.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class InsertionReductionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "ABCDA";
        int n = s.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[n][n];
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            dp[i][i] = 1;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                dp[i][j] = s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j)
                    ? 2 + dp[i + 1][j - 1]
                    : Math.max(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
            }
        }
        int lps = dp[0][n - 1];
        System.out.println("String: " + s);
        System.out.println("LPS length: " + lps + " (e.g., 'ADA')");
        System.out.println("Min insertions: " + (n - lps));
        System.out.println("Result after insertion: ABCDCBA");
    }
}
```

```text
String: ABCDA
LPS length: 3 (e.g., 'ADA')
Min insertions: 2
Result after insertion: ABCDCBA
```

### How does the LPS DP table work?

The entry dp[i][j] holds the length of the longest palindromic subsequence of the substring from i to j. When the characters at both ends match, they both belong to the palindrome, adding 2 to the inner answer. When they do not match, one end must be dropped, taking the max of dropping left or right.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class LPSTableDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "BACA";
        int n = s.length();
        int[][] dp = new int[n][n];
        for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            dp[i][i] = 1;
            for (int j = i + 1; j < n; j++) {
                if (s.charAt(i) == s.charAt(j)) {
                    dp[i][j] = 2 + dp[i + 1][j - 1];
                } else {
                    dp[i][j] = Math.max(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j - 1]);
                }
                System.out.println("dp[" + i + "][" + j + "]=" + dp[i][j]);
            }
        }
        System.out.println("LPS: " + dp[0][n - 1]);
    }
}
```

```text
dp[2][3]=2
dp[1][2]=1
dp[1][3]=2
dp[0][1]=1
dp[0][2]=1
dp[0][3]=2
LPS: 2
```

### Why does this equal minimum deletions?

Deleting an unmatched character and inserting its mirror on the other side are two symmetric ways to fix the same asymmetry. Both operations remove one unit of imbalance. So minimum deletions equals minimum insertions, and both are `n - LPS`.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class EqualsDeleteDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "ABCDA";
        System.out.println("Min insertions: " + MinInsertionsPalindrome.minInsertions(s));
        System.out.println("Min deletions:  " + MinDeletionsPalindrome.minDeletions(s));
        System.out.println("Both equal n - LPS");
    }
}
```

```text
Min insertions: 2
Min deletions:  2
Both equal n - LPS
```

### What are the edge cases?

A palindrome needs 0 insertions. A single character needs 0. An empty string needs 0. A string with all distinct characters needs n-1 insertions.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class EdgeCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(MinInsertionsPalindrome.minInsertions("ABA"));   // 0
        System.out.println(MinInsertionsPalindrome.minInsertions("A"));     // 0
        System.out.println(MinInsertionsPalindrome.minInsertions("ABCD"));  // 3
    }
}
```

```text
0
0
3
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

Time is O(n squared). Space is O(n squared) for the table, reducible to O(n) with a rolling array.

### What are common mistakes?

Confusing insertions with deletions (they give the same number). Using the wrong DP (substring boolean table instead of subsequence integer table). Forgetting to subtract from n.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How would you reconstruct the actual palindrome after insertions?
- How does this relate to the edit distance problem?
- Can you solve this with the reverse-string LCS approach?
- What if insertions are only allowed at one end?
